  "flashcards": [
    {
      "id": "mlf-fc01",
      "front": "Slope-intercept form",
      "back": "y = mx + b. m = slope, b = y-intercept (where the line crosses the y-axis). Easiest form to graph."
    },
    {
      "id": "mlf-fc02",
      "front": "Standard form",
      "back": "Ax + By = C, with whole numbers and A usually positive. Easy to find both intercepts."
    },
    {
      "id": "mlf-fc03",
      "front": "Point-slope form",
      "back": "y − y₁ = m(x − x₁). Use it when you know a point (x₁, y₁) and the slope m. Watch the signs: y + 1 means y₁ = −1."
    },
    {
      "id": "mlf-fc04",
      "front": "Slope formula",
      "back": "m = (y₂ − y₁) / (x₂ − x₁) = rise ÷ run. Up is +, down is −; right is +."
    },
    {
      "id": "mlf-fc05",
      "front": "Intercepts from standard form",
      "back": "x-intercept: set y = 0 and solve. y-intercept: set x = 0 and solve. Example: 3x + 4y = 12 → (4, 0) and (0, 3)."
    },
    {
      "id": "mlf-fc06",
      "front": "Slope from standard form",
      "back": "Solve for y, or use the shortcut slope = −A/B. Example: 2x − 5y = 10 → slope 2/5."
    },
    {
      "id": "mlf-fc07",
      "front": "Point-slope → slope-intercept",
      "back": "Distribute, then get y alone. y − 3 = 4(x + 2) → y − 3 = 4x + 8 → y = 4x + 11."
    },
    {
      "id": "mlf-fc08",
      "front": "Slope-intercept → standard",
      "back": "Clear fractions (multiply by the denominator), move the x-term left, make A positive. y = (2/3)x − 4 → 2x − 3y = 12."
    },
    {
      "id": "mlf-fc09",
      "front": "Line from two points",
      "back": "1) Find m with the slope formula. 2) Plug m and either point into point-slope (or into y = mx + b to find b)."
    },
    {
      "id": "mlf-fc10",
      "front": "Reading a graph",
      "back": "Find b where the line crosses the y-axis. Pick two clear grid points, count rise and run for m. Then write y = mx + b."
    }
  ],
